    "display_name": "The Raven",
    "long_instructions": "Find n-grams in a sentence from *The Raven* by Edgar Allan Poe, where `n` can be any number you choose.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n- Complete the `get_ngrams` function. Given a list of tokens, and a value for `n` the function should return a list of n-grams.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nA list slice like `tokens[1:4]` grabs the items from index 1 up to (but not including) index 4, and gives them back as a new list.\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Once\", \"upon\", \"a\", \"midnight\"]\r\nprint(tokens[1:3])    # ['upon', 'a']\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd the `tuple` function can be used to turn a `list` into a `tuple`.\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Once\", \"upon\", \"a\", \"midnight\"]\r\nprint(tuple(tokens))    # ('Once', 'upon', 'a', 'midnight')\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo these can be combined to create a tuple of a slice of a list:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Once\", \"upon\", \"a\", \"midnight\"]\r\nprint(tuple(tokens[1:3]))    # ('upon', 'a')\r\n```",
